==Aragonese==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|an|la|stō|stāre|t=stand}}, from PIE {{m|ine-pro|*steh₂-}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{an-pr}}

===Verb===
{{head|an|verb}}

# to [[be]]

====Usage notes====
Contrary to other Iberian languages, Aragonese only makes use of one verb "to be", this means that the verb "ser" is not present.

====Conjugation====
{{an-conj-estar}}

==Catalan==

===Etymology===
{{inh+|ca|la|sto|stāre|t=to stand}}. Where the velar infix that is characteristic for the second conjugation comes from is unknown.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ca-IPA}}
* {{audio|ca|LL-Q7026 (cat)-Unjoanqualsevol-estar.wav}}
* {{rhymes|ca|a(ɾ)|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{ca-verb}}

# {{lb|ca|transitive|copular}} to [[be]]; to [[currently]] [[be]] in a [[state]] or have a [[characteristic]] {{gl|Used to connect a noun to an adjective that describes a temporary state of being.}}
#: {{ux|ca|'''Estic''' cansada.|I '''am''' tired.|inline=1}}
# {{lb|ca|auxiliary}} {{n-g|forms the [[continuous]] [[aspect]], together with a [[present participle]]}}
#: {{ux|ca|Ja '''està''' dormint.|He '''is''' already sleeping.|inline=1}}
# {{lb|ca|intransitive|+adverbial phrase}} to live or spending time.
#: {{ux|ca|M’'''estic''' a una petita pensió vora el mar.|I '''am''' in a small boarding house on the seashore.|inline=1}}

====Usage notes====
* This is one of two verbs that can be translated as ''to be'', the other being {{m|ca|ser}}/{{m|ca|ésser}}. ''Ser/ésser'' indicates something that is inherent and not expected to change, whereas {{m|ca|estar}} describes temporary qualities that apply only at a particular time. ''Ser/ésser'' relates to {{m|ca|estar}} as {{m|en|essence}} relates to {{m|en|state}}, etymologically as well as semantically.

