==Galician==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|gl|roa-opt|estar}}, from {{inh|gl|la|stō|stāre|t=stand}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{gl-pr|ėstar}}
* {{hyph|gl|es|tar}}

===Verb===
{{gl-verb|reinteg=1}}

# to [[be]]
# {{lb|gl|copulative}} to [[be]] {{gl|denotes a [[transient]] quality; a quality expected to change}} {{+obj|gl|adjective}}
# {{lb|gl|auxiliary}} to [[be]]; {{n-g|forms the [[progressive]] aspect}} {{+obj|gl|ger/:a(inf)}}
# {{lb|gl|intransitive}} to [[cost]] {{gl|to be worth a certain amount of money}}, especially of something whose price changes often {{+obj|gl|:a<price>}}
#: {{uxi|gl|Os ovos '''están a''' 2€ a ducia|Eggs '''cost''' 2€ a dozen.}}
# {{lb|gl|intransitive}} to [[be]] about to; {{n-g|indicates imminence or }} {{+obj|gl|:para(inf)}}
#* {{quote-book|gl|year=1861|translator=José Sánchez de Santa María|title=Gospel of Matthew|passage=Pois así tamén, cando vosoutros víredes todas estas cousas, tede por certo que xa o Fillo do home '''está para''' chegar, e que xa está á porta|translation=Even so you also, when you see all these things, know that the Son of Man '''is about to''' arrive, even at the doors.}}
# to [[stand]]

====Usage notes====
Like Portuguese and Spanish, Galician has two different verbs that are usually translated to English as “to be”. The verb {{m|gl|ser}} relates to essence, origin, or physical description. In contrast, the verb {{m|gl|estar}} relates to current state or position.

